.TH TPA 1 "August 2026" "TPA Manual" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpa \- build Debian packages and verified APT repository trees
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpa
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpa
creates Debian package directory trees, delegates archive operations to
.BR dpkg-deb (1),
and derives APT repository metadata from actual .deb artifacts.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Create a package tree with DEBIAN/control and maintainer scripts.
.TP
.B build
Build a .deb package with dpkg-deb.
.TP
.B parse
Read control metadata from a .deb package.
.TP
.B pack
Generate and verify Packages, Packages.gz, Release, and optionally InRelease.
.TP
.B json
Read package initialization configuration from standard input.
.TP
.B schema
Print the TypeScript-style JSON configuration interface.
.SH IMPORTANT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -in " path"
Input package tree, .deb path, or directory of .deb artifacts, depending on the command.
.TP
.BI -out " path"
Non-atomic output path.
.TP
.BI -atomic-publish " path"
For pack, build and verify a sibling staging tree and atomically publish it at path on Linux.
Use this mode when replacing a repository that may be read concurrently.
.TP
.BI -gpg " selector"
Select one GPG secret key. Full fingerprints are recommended. Empty means unsigned.
TPA uses the caller's GPG environment, including GNUPGHOME.
.TP
.BI -depends " relationships"
Set Depends in a generated package control file.
.TP
.BI -pre-depends " relationships"
Set Pre-Depends.
.TP
.BI -recommends " relationships"
Set Recommends.
.TP
.BI -suggests " relationships"
Set Suggests.
.TP
.BI -provides " relationships"
Set Provides.
.TP
.BI -conflicts " relationships"
Set Conflicts.
.TP
.BI -breaks " relationships"
Set Breaks.
.TP
.BI -replaces " relationships"
Set Replaces.
.SH REPOSITORY CORRECTNESS
The .deb artifacts are authoritative package state. Packages, Release, and InRelease
are derived state; no persistent package database is required. The package control stanza
is read from each actual .deb and preserved in Packages. Package-provided Filename, Size,
and SHA256 fields are replaced by values derived from the published artifact.
TPA verifies package bytes and Release index hashes before success. Signed repositories
additionally require a valid InRelease signature from the selected full fingerprint and
a signed payload identical to Release.
.PP
Package identity is Package plus Version plus Architecture. Byte-identical duplicate
artifacts are indexed once. Conflicting artifacts with the same identity fail generation.
.PP
The input artifact directory is the desired repository snapshot. An artifact included in
a fresh input snapshot is included in the generated repository; an omitted artifact is
absent. Historical versions remain available only while their .deb artifacts remain in
that input set. Use a new or empty direct output path for exact snapshot generation.
.SH ATOMIC PUBLICATION
Atomic publication uses a fresh sibling staging directory, complete generation and
verification, a Linux rename exchange, and replaced-tree cleanup. Failure before exchange
leaves the previous live repository unchanged. Repository data is published readable but
not writable by readers; GPG key material is not stored in the repository tree.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tpa init -name=meta -ver=1.0 -arch=all \
  -maintainer='Example <example@example.invalid>' \
  -desc='Meta package' -depends='base (= 1.0)' -out=build/meta

tpa build -in=build/meta -out=dist/meta_1.0_all.deb

tpa pack -in=dist -atomic-publish=/srv/apt/example \
  -gpg=FULL_SIGNING_FINGERPRINT
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Invalid input, unknown commands, build failures, verification
failures, and signing failures return non-zero. Diagnostics are written to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dpkg-deb (1),
.BR apt-get (8),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR gpg (1)
